faqs:
  - q: "How do I know if my smoke alarm has expired?"
    a: "Twist it off the ceiling and look at the back — there's a manufacture date, and often an expiry or 'replace by' date. Ten years from manufacture is the limit, no matter the type or brand. No date visible at all? That usually means it's old enough that the answer is yes. Yellowed plastic is another giveaway: white alarms tan with age like old computer cases, and a properly yellow alarm is well past its use-by."
  - q: "The alarm still beeps when I press the test button. Doesn't that mean it works?"
    a: "Sadly, no — this is the most common misunderstanding we hear. The test button checks the battery, the electronics and the sounder. It does not check whether the smoke sensor itself can still detect smoke, and it's the sensor that wears out with age as dust builds up and components drift. A 15-year-old alarm can pass its button test every week and still sleep straight through actual smoke."
  - q: "Why does my alarm chirp at night, and can you make it stop?"
    a: "The classic 3am chirp is a low-battery warning, and there's real physics behind the timing — batteries put out slightly less voltage when cold, so the alarm tips over its low-power threshold in the small hours when the house is coldest. If the alarm is under 10 years old, a fresh battery fixes it. If it's older, replace the whole unit — with a sealed 10-year alarm, and that chirp leaves your life for a decade."
  - q: "What do you do with the old alarms?"
    a: "We take them away. Old ionisation alarms contain a tiny sealed radioactive source — completely safe in use, but not something for the kitchen bin. We dispose of them through the proper channels, so your only job is enjoying the silence."
